Progress status for this period: regressing. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your body is telling you it needs rest — a deload this week is the right move.",
  "summary": "This was a tough period, and the data backs up exactly how you're feeling. Strength dropped across every single muscle group — chest, back, legs, and shoulders all fell by 5–7 points, which is a clear and consistent signal that your body is under-recovered. Combined with your joint aches, that run-down feeling, and sessions getting progressively worse, this is textbook accumulative fatigue. The good news is that this is completely fixable with a planned, intentional deload. You haven't lost your fitness — your nervous system and connective tissue just need a chance to catch up with the hard work you've been putting in.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "Every muscle group regressed this period, which is what triggered the regressing status. Chest dropped from 105 to 99 (down 6 points), back from 104 to 98 (down 6 points), legs from 104 to 97 (down 7 points), shoulders from 103 to 99 (down 4 points), biceps from 101 to 100 (down 1 point), and triceps from 102 to 100 (down 2 points). The largest drops are in the big compound-dominant groups — legs, back, and chest — which are also the most systemically demanding. This universal decline, hitting all groups simultaneously, is not a technique or programming issue. It is the hallmark of accumulated systemic fatigue that is suppressing performance across the board. Continuing to push through at current loads is likely to deepen the regression and increase injury risk.",
    "status": "regressing"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at just 4% of total training volume (6 sets), roughly half the allocation of triceps at 8.4% (6 sets at higher load). For a muscle-building goal, ensuring balanced arm development over time is worthwhile, though this is a lower priority than addressing overall fatigue right now."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Leg volume at 22.4% is somewhat lower than chest (26.8%) and back (28%), and legs also showed the steepest strength index drop this period (down 7 points). Once recovered, bringing leg volume closer to parity with the upper body pushing and pulling groups would support balanced muscle development."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "All six muscle groups declined in strength this period, with the three largest movers — legs, back, and chest — dropping 6–7 points each. The user is also reporting joint aches, persistent fatigue, and deteriorating session quality, all of which are well-established signs of accumulated fatigue. A deload, typically structured as 1 week at roughly 40–60% of normal volume and 10–20% reduced load, is strongly indicated to allow recovery and set the stage for renewed progress.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Take a full deload this coming period: keep the same movement patterns but reduce your sets to about 40–50% of your usual volume and lower the weight by around 10–20%. The goal is to move, not to challenge.",
    "Prioritise sleep and nutrition during the deload — this is when the actual recovery happens. Aim for at least 7–8 hours of sleep per night and make sure you are eating enough protein (roughly 1.6–2.2g per kg of bodyweight, so around 115–160g per day at your current weight).",
    "After the deload, reintroduce leg volume gradually. Legs showed the largest drop this period and have been slightly under-served in volume relative to chest and back — bringing them up to 25–28% of total volume over the coming weeks will support your muscle-building goal more evenly.",
    "Once you return to full training, consider building in a planned lighter week every 4–6 weeks so that fatigue does not accumulate to this level again before it affects performance."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Universal strength regression across all muscle groups (legs -7, back -6, chest -6, shoulders -4) combined with reported joint pain and fatigue confirmed accumulated systemic fatigue; a full deload was recommended before resuming progressive loading."
}
